Brief Note:
Given a sorted array and a target value, the task is to:

Return the index of the target if it exists.
If it does not exist, return the index where it should be inserted so the array remains sorted.

This problem is efficiently solved using Binary Search by repeatedly narrowing the search range.
When the target is not found, the algorithm ends with the pointer l positioned at the correct insertion index, which is why returning l works.

Time Complexity:
O(log n)
→ The search space is halved in every iteration.

Space Complexity:
O(1)
→ Only constant extra variables (l, h, mid) are used.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Solution {
    public int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {
        int l=0;
        int h=nums.length-1;
        while(l<=h){
            int m=l+(h-l)/2;
            if(nums[m]==target){
                return m;
            }else if(nums[m]<target){
                l=m+1;
            }else{
                h=m-1;
            }
        }
        return l;
        
    }
}
